{
  "_note": "The world's pieces, drawn back to front in the order of `layers`, then in the order listed here. Positions and sizes are logical pixels of the 1440 × 900 design, which the world scales to the window. A piece with an `image` (a file path under src/world/art/) shows that image stretched to its size; without one, or when the image fails to load, the world draws a placeholder shape in its `color`, lit as `light` says. An SVG image is drawn once, when it loads, at `artResolution` pixels per logical pixel of its own size. The street's scenery (`backdrop` pieces, #198) is art cut from the design reference, each piece at the reference's place: the five triplexes, with a mirrored copy of the first and copies of the fifth past the design's edges for wide windows; the sidewalk, the curb and the road in tiles one design wide; the sky's gradient stretched, its top colour above the design. A backdrop whose image fails to load is a flat shape in its `color`. The truck (#199) is art cut from the same reference, at its place and in its paint order: the body, the espresso machine, the croissant rack, the croissants (the reference's, on three shelves), the counter, the awning and the sign's board, and its shadow on the road, a backdrop under the people's. The counter's image is the strip of the truck in front of the avatar, the counter with the body and the interior behind it, so it hides the avatar's legs and lines up with the truck. The owner, the business name and the lettering stay in code. `shows` keeps a piece to some days: `open` or `closed` ones; those a change to the street from an event card is in effect, `{ \"event\": <the card's id> }`, except the changes its choices listed in an optional `except` made, such as the move away from the cones; those a product cannot be sold, `{ \"unavailable\": <the product> }`; or open ones it can be sold, `{ \"sells\": <the product> }`, such as the steam over the espresso machine. `text` is what a sign or a board says, in French: the road sign, the rival truck, the banner, the « Hors service » card and the truck's lettering each need one, and no other piece takes one. A `lettering` is written in Fredoka in its colour, as tall as its box and centred in it. `fans` sets who fans themselves while it `shows`, as in a heat wave: each customer who comes then holds a fan of `color` with `chance`, which swings `swing` radians each way and back once every `periodMs` real milliseconds. The « MENU » chalkboard (#208) is cut from concept B of the reference (ui_concepts.py), listed with the scenery, before what the events put on the street, such as the road sign, which stands in front of it. `fill` makes the croissants follow the stock: `capacity` units fill the rack, shown in `steps` shelves. The `light.shadowLayer` holds no piece: the world draws the shadows on the ground there, under what stands on it. `spots` are the things in the street the player clicks to open their panel (#208), each a box of the design that opens a dock button, on a tab of « Le camion » if `opens` names one, with a label and its detail in French, over the box or under it with `labelBelow`. A spot shows with its `piece`, or on the days its own `shows` says, or always; a later spot is on top of an earlier one. `streetLegend` is the note that the street can be clicked, in the player's first game for its first `days` days, centred on `x` with its foot at `y`.",
